## Techniques not applied (process output)

- trade-off — not applicable — this is a claim-verification task with no decision among named discrete options to weight and score; there is nothing to recommend choosing between, only a compound factual/economic claim to confirm or refute
- fishbone — not applicable — inversion (Phase 2) adequately surfaced the hidden-assumption space for this two-part comparative claim without needing a breadth-first cause-category brainstorm

## §6→§4 closure ledger (process output)

- "An 85% round-trip electrical efficiency at 290-565°C is not merely unachieved but thermodynamically impossible [Case A]" → chain C1 ✓
- "85% is not a law violation but is unsupported by any real, near-term, or theoretical-model figure [Case B]" → chain C2 ✓
- "The 85%-class figures actually circulating in industry (Malta's 85-95%) are traceable to a combined-heat-and-power accounting basis, not the electricity-only basis the claim describes" → chain C3 ✓
- "A 5 MWh molten-salt/power-cycle system costs several times more per kWh of nameplate capacity than a same-size lithium-ion system under current technology and market structure" → chain C4 ✓
- "The claim's surface plausibility comes from merging a CHP-based efficiency figure with a resistive-heated architecture whose physics caps electricity-only round-trip efficiency at ≈64%" → chain C1, chain C3 ✓
- "Two named, bounded uncertainties remain: Malta's eventual full-scale demonstration, and future maturation of sub-MW power-cycle equipment markets" → chain C2, chain C4 ✓
- "Whether thermal storage is competitive at larger scale or under a cycle/duration-adjusted cost basis is left out of scope, not settled as a 'no'" → chain C4 (see also section 5) ✓

All section-6 claims trace to a named section-4 chain; no claim was cut.

